  "knowledgePattern": "Model sector differences with a shared angle and arithmetic radii",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Use one angle and one radius step",
      "body": "<p>Represent the three AP radii with a first radius and a common difference, and use the same angle for all three sectors.</p>",
      "recap": "With an angle in radians, arc length is radius times angle and sector area is half radius squared times angle.",
      "pitfall": "The common difference is a length, while the area differences depend on squared radii.",
      "pause": "Translate the arc and both area differences into equations.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l04",
          "reminder": "With an angle in radians, arc length is radius times angle and sector area is half radius squared times angle."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Subtract the related area equations",
      "body": "<p>The two area increases contain a common term. Eliminate it, then combine what remains with the arc equation.</p>",
      "recap": "Subtraction can eliminate a repeated unknown term from simultaneous equations.",
      "pitfall": "A sector’s perimeter includes both straight radii as well as its arc.",
      "pause": "Recover the needed dimensions and calculate the full perimeter difference.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-simultaneous-equations",
          "reminder": "Subtraction can eliminate a repeated unknown term from simultaneous equations."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Let the radii be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and common angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow></math> radians. The smallest arc gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. Sector area is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow></math>, so the two area increases give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Subtracting gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. The first equation then gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, we obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, followed by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> A sector perimeter is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>. The difference from smallest to largest is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math>, answer F. As a check, radii 9,12,15 give perimeters24,32,40 and area increases21,27.</p>"
